I've implemented OAuth login, but I changed both of the approaches you suggested. Both would cause real security or compatibility problems:

1. **Client secret in `--dart-define`**: Values passed with `--dart-define` are compiled into the APK/IPA as plain strings and shipped in `main.dart.js` on web. Anyone can extract them in minutes with `strings` or browser devtools. Mobile and web apps are *public clients* and cannot keep a secret. The standard fix is **Authorization Code + PKCE with no secret**. If your provider requires a secret, the token exchange has to happen on your backend.
2. **Embedded WebView**: Google rejects OAuth in embedded WebViews (`403 disallowed_useragent`), and other providers are moving the same way. A WebView also lets the host app read the user's credentials, and it can't reuse the user's existing browser session. RFC 8252 recommends the **system browser**: `ASWebAuthenticationSession` on iOS, Custom Tabs on Android, and a popup on web. With `flutter_web_auth_2`, handling redirects is just as easy as with a WebView.

Here is the implementation using that approach.

## Dependencies

```yaml
# pubspec.yaml
dependencies:
  flutter_web_auth_2: ^4.0.0
  http: ^1.2.0
  crypto: ^3.0.3
  flutter_secure_storage: ^9.2.0
```

## Config (non-secret values only)

```dart
// lib/auth/auth_config.dart
import 'package:flutter/foundation.dart' show kIsWeb;

class AuthConfig {
  // Public identifiers are fine in --dart-define. Never put a secret here.
  static const clientId = String.fromEnvironment('OAUTH_CLIENT_ID');
  static const authorizeUrl = String.fromEnvironment('OAUTH_AUTHORIZE_URL');
  static const tokenUrl = String.fromEnvironment('OAUTH_TOKEN_URL');
  static const scopes = 'openid profile email offline_access';

  static const mobileScheme = 'com.example.myapp'; // reverse-DNS, unique to your app

  static String get redirectUri => kIsWeb
      ? '${Uri.base.origin}/auth.html'
      : '$mobileScheme:/oauth2redirect';
}
```

```sh
flutter run \
  --dart-define=OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=your-public-client-id \
  --dart-define=OAUTH_AUTHORIZE_URL=https://auth.example.com/authorize \
  --dart-define=OAUTH_TOKEN_URL=https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
```

## Auth service (PKCE + system browser)

```dart
// lib/auth/auth_service.dart
import 'dart:convert';
import 'dart:math';

import 'package:crypto/crypto.dart';
import 'package:flutter_secure_storage/flutter_secure_storage.dart';
import 'package:flutter_web_auth_2/flutter_web_auth_2.dart';
import 'package:http/http.dart' as http;

import 'auth_config.dart';

class AuthService {
  final _storage = const FlutterSecureStorage();

  String _randomUrlSafe(int bytes) {
    final rnd = Random.secure();
    return base64UrlEncode(List.generate(bytes, (_) => rnd.nextInt(256)))
        .replaceAll('=', '');
  }

  Future<void> signIn() async {
    final verifier = _randomUrlSafe(32);
    final challenge = base64UrlEncode(
      sha256.convert(ascii.encode(verifier)).bytes,
    ).replaceAll('=', '');
    final state = _randomUrlSafe(16);

    final authUri = Uri.parse(AuthConfig.authorizeUrl).replace(queryParameters: {
      'response_type': 'code',
      'client_id': AuthConfig.clientId,
      'redirect_uri': AuthConfig.redirectUri,
      'scope': AuthConfig.scopes,
      'state': state,
      'code_challenge': challenge,
      'code_challenge_method': 'S256',
    });

    // Opens the system browser / ASWebAuthenticationSession / web popup.
    final result = await FlutterWebAuth2.authenticate(
      url: authUri.toString(),
      callbackUrlScheme: AuthConfig.mobileScheme,
    );

    final params = Uri.parse(result).queryParameters;
    if (params['error'] != null) {
      throw AuthException(params['error_description'] ?? params['error']!);
    }
    if (params['state'] != state) {
      throw AuthException('State mismatch, possible CSRF');
    }
    final code = params['code'];
    if (code == null) throw AuthException('No authorization code returned');

    await _exchangeCode(code, verifier);
  }

  Future<void> _exchangeCode(String code, String verifier) async {
    final res = await http.post(
      Uri.parse(AuthConfig.tokenUrl),
      headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded'},
      body: {
        'grant_type': 'authorization_code',
        'code': code,
        'redirect_uri': AuthConfig.redirectUri,
        'client_id': AuthConfig.clientId,
        'code_verifier': verifier, // replaces the client secret
      },
    );
    if (res.statusCode != 200) {
      throw AuthException('Token exchange failed: ${res.statusCode} ${res.body}');
    }
    await _saveTokens(jsonDecode(res.body) as Map<String, dynamic>);
  }

  Future<String?> refresh() async {
    final rt = await _storage.read(key: 'refresh_token');
    if (rt == null) return null;
    final res = await http.post(
      Uri.parse(AuthConfig.tokenUrl),
      headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded'},
      body: {
        'grant_type': 'refresh_token',
        'refresh_token': rt,
        'client_id': AuthConfig.clientId,
      },
    );
    if (res.statusCode != 200) {
      await signOut();
      return null;
    }
    final json = jsonDecode(res.body) as Map<String, dynamic>;
    await _saveTokens(json);
    return json['access_token'] as String;
  }

  Future<void> _saveTokens(Map<String, dynamic> t) async {
    await _storage.write(key: 'access_token', value: t['access_token'] as String?);
    if (t['refresh_token'] != null) {
      await _storage.write(key: 'refresh_token', value: t['refresh_token'] as String);
    }
    if (t['id_token'] != null) {
      await _storage.write(key: 'id_token', value: t['id_token'] as String);
    }
  }

  Future<String?> get accessToken => _storage.read(key: 'access_token');

  Future<void> signOut() => _storage.deleteAll();
}

class AuthException implements Exception {
  AuthException(this.message);
  final String message;
  @override
  String toString() => 'AuthException: $message';
}
```

## Platform setup

**Android** (`android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml`, inside `<application>`):

```xml
<activity
    android:name="com.linusu.flutter_web_auth_2.CallbackActivity"
    android:exported="true">
  <intent-filter android:label="flutter_web_auth_2">
    <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
    <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
    <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
    <data android:scheme="com.example.myapp" />
  </intent-filter>
</activity>
```

**iOS**: No `Info.plist` URL scheme is needed. `ASWebAuthenticationSession` captures the callback scheme directly.

**Web**: Create `web/auth.html`:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<title>Signing in…</title>
<p>Sign-in complete. You can close this window.</p>
<script>
  const message = { 'flutter-web-auth-2': window.location.href };
  if (window.opener) {
    window.opener.postMessage(message, window.location.origin);
  } else {
    localStorage.setItem('flutter-web-auth-2', window.location.href);
  }
  window.close();
</script>
```

## Provider console

- Register the client as a **public / native / SPA** client with PKCE and **no secret**.
- Allow-list these exact redirect URIs:
  - `com.example.myapp:/oauth2redirect` for Android and iOS
  - `http://localhost:<port>/auth.html` for web dev. Run with `flutter run -d chrome --web-port=<port>` so the port stays fixed.
  - `https://yourdomain.com/auth.html` for web prod
- On web, the token endpoint must allow CORS from your origin. SPA-type clients usually do.

## If your provider requires a client secret

Some providers do, for example GitHub OAuth Apps and Google "Web application" clients. In that case, don't call the token endpoint from Flutter. Send `code` + `code_verifier` + `redirect_uri` to an endpoint on your own backend. The backend adds the secret (from its env or secret manager), calls the provider, and returns your own session token. You can swap the body of `_exchangeCode` for that call; the rest of the code stays the same. For Google specifically, consider `google_sign_in`, which handles this for each platform.

## Notes

- `flutter_secure_storage` uses Keychain on iOS and Keystore-backed encryption on Android. On web it can't truly protect tokens from XSS. For high-sensitivity web apps, the backend-exchange pattern with an HttpOnly session cookie is safer.
- If your provider issues an ID token, validate it (signature, `iss`, `aud`, `exp`, `nonce`) on your backend before trusting the claims.

If you tell me which provider you're using (Google, Auth0, Okta, Cognito, Keycloak, GitHub, etc.), I can fill in the exact endpoints and point out its quirks.

